= Measurement =

== Overview ==
'''Measurement''' is a structured process for obtaining a result concerning a property or quantity by relating a target to procedures, instruments, models, reference systems, units, and calibration.

Measurement is central to science and engineering because it connects theoretical or practical quantities with empirical observations.

An instrument reading is not automatically a complete measurement result.

== Measurand ==
The '''measurand''' is the quantity intended to be measured.

Examples:
* length of a rod;
* mass of a sample;
* voltage across a component;
* concentration of a molecule;
* temperature of a fluid.

A measurand needs sufficient specification.

"The temperature" can be ambiguous unless location, time, physical system, and measurement conditions are clear.

== Measurement process ==
A measurement chain can include:

<pre>
target property
→ physical interaction
→ sensor response
→ instrument indication
→ calibration/model
→ processed value
→ measurement result
</pre>

Each stage can introduce uncertainty or bias.

== Measurement result ==
A measurement result can include more than a single number.

Relevant information may include:
* measured value;
* unit;
* [[Uncertainty|measurement uncertainty]];
* calibration state;
* method;
* environmental conditions;
* traceability/reference information.

Without context, a numerical result may be difficult to interpret or compare.

== Units and reference systems ==
Units make quantity values comparable.

Modern metrology uses the International System of Units (SI), whose units are defined through fixed values of fundamental constants.

Measurement traceability connects results through an unbroken documented chain of calibrations to appropriate references.

Traceability supports comparability across instruments, laboratories, and time.

== Calibration ==
'''Calibration''' establishes a relation between known/reference values and the indications or outputs of a measuring system.

Calibration can reveal:
* offset;
* scale;
* nonlinearity;
* drift;
* sensitivity.

Calibration is not identical to adjustment. An instrument can be calibrated without being physically changed.

== Accuracy and precision ==
[[Accuracy and precision]] describe different properties.

Accuracy concerns closeness to an appropriate reference or true quantity value.

Precision concerns agreement among repeated measurements under specified conditions.

A measurement process can be precise but systematically biased.

== Error ==
Measurement '''error''' is conceptually the difference between a measured quantity value and a reference/true quantity value.

The exact true value is often unknown.

Therefore uncertainty, rather than exact error, is usually what can be quantified in a real measurement.

== Measurement uncertainty ==
Measurement uncertainty describes the dispersion of quantity values that could reasonably be attributed to the measurand given available information.

Sources can include:
* calibration;
* repeatability;
* environmental conditions;
* instrument resolution;
* sampling;
* data processing;
* model assumptions.

Uncertainty evaluation makes limitations explicit rather than pretending the measured value is exact.

== Observation and theory ==
Measurements are model-mediated.

Turning an instrument signal into a quantity value can require:
* physical theory;
* calibration equations;
* signal processing;
* background subtraction;
* classification;
* statistical inference.

This is why [[Empirical evidence|empirical evidence]] can depend on theoretical assumptions without becoming arbitrary.

== Measurement validity ==
A precisely measured variable can still be the wrong variable for the scientific question.

For example, a questionnaire may consistently measure a response pattern while failing to represent the intended psychological construct.

This connects measurement to [[Validation|validation]].

== Repeatability and reproducibility ==
Metrology distinguishes measurement conditions.

'''Repeatability''' concerns measurements under closely matched conditions.

'''Reproducibility conditions''' involve changed conditions such as operators, instruments, laboratories, or locations.

This use of "reproducibility" is older and different from the computational-science convention discussed in [[Reproducibility]].

== Epistemic notes ==
Measurement is not simply "reading a number from an instrument." A defensible measurement result depends on a defined measurand, method, model, reference system, calibration, and uncertainty.

